                   REGISTER ALLOCATION HISTORY AND PERSPECTIVE

The register allocator is a part of the encoder that has evolved, and will 
continue to evolve. This document gives an overview of where the register
allocator has been, and where it is going.

THE 1982 ALGORITHIM
-------------------

The first compiler in 1980 didn't perform register allocation at all. This was
replaced in 1982 by the first allocator, running under the Z80 compiler.

The first allocator kept track of registers via an array of descriptors that
kept track of things such as register in use, what the contents of the register
was (as a type pointer), logical age of when it was last used, etc.

Registers were allocated as needed within the code flow. If a register was 
needed, it was allocated just before use, then released afterwards.

The context within a register determined if an actual load to the register
needed to occur, or if the previous contents could be used.

There existed calls for allocation of any register, a specific register, and
also for register pairs. The allocator worked very hard to move, copy or
rearrange registers to make specific allocations.

PROS

The big advantage of the first algoritim is that it was self contained. A 
particular operation, say add, would allocate and free registers according to 
its needs.

CONS

The algoritim was short sighted, and would thrash on occasion to get registers
in the proper arrangement. Also, with the very limited number of 16 bit Z80 
registers, it was never possible to prove that trashing would always resolve
of its own accord, although this was never observed to fail.

FINAL ANALYSIS

The 1982 algorithim is no longer in use. It was a good and simple algoritim
which resulted in observably good code. Although it generated false moves,
these were observed to be rare and not serious.

THE 2004 ALGORITIM
------------------

With the I80386 compiler, the processing of the intermediate was based on work
on an internal tree structure of the code. The algoritim is based on two passes
over the internal intermediate. The first assigns registers to each 
intermediate. The second actually generates the code.